============================================================================
  SUMMARY -- three baselines, 60 tasks
============================================================================

  fully-solved tasks (all tests pass):
    Reference    : 60 / 60
    Unguided rnd : ~  0 / 6000 sample-tasks (across 100 seeds each)
    Claude       : 60 / 60

  per-test-case pass rate:
    Reference    : 180/180 (100.0%)
    Claude       : 180/180 (100.0%)

  verdict: STRONG WIN on capability-gap quantification
    -> Claude can write LOVA from prompts at ~100% pass rate
    -> Unguided random is essentially useless (~0% task coverage)
    -> The gap is the whole range -- motivates fine-tuning

  Caveat, and it is load-bearing (Exp 13, F3):
  LOVABench's tasks were authored *in* the language, and the
  v2 prompts state the formula outright -- "Compute
  p(tau(sigma(n)))".  So the Claude baseline measures
  transcription into s-expressions, not program synthesis.
  Quote it as evidence that the surface is writable, not
  that an LLM can program in LOVA.
